>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `register_instance`

``` hol4
linarithData.register_instance :
  linarithData.linarith_instance -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Installs a carrier implementation in the session-local linarith registry.

An instance record identifies its `ty` and whether its order is
`discrete`. Its `dest` component recognises addition, optional subtraction
and negation, multiplication, optional division and successor, literals,
strict and non-strict order, and constructs literals. Its `kit` component
provides monotonicity, strict-order, negated-order, disequality, and
non-negativity theorems used during replay.

The remaining fields are a normalising conversion, `pre_split` rules,
`atom_facts`, and optional `divmod_facts`. These callbacks extend the
generic preprocessing and replay engine without adding carrier tests to
it. Injections between carriers are installed separately with
`register_injection`.

The registry is keyed by `#ty`. Registering another instance for the same
type atomically replaces the old entry and issues a warning. Registration
does not eagerly validate the theorem kit; malformed components fail when
an arithmetic operation consumes them. Instance modules normally register
their records as a module-loading side effect.

### Failure

Registration itself performs no theorem-kit validation. Invalid callbacks
or replay theorems generally fail only when the instance is used.

### Example

The natural-number instance can be reinstalled explicitly. Calling the
resulting function after `linarithLib` has loaded intentionally warns that
it replaces the existing entry.

``` hol4
fun reinstall_num_instance () =
  linarithData.register_instance linarithNum.instance;
```

